TEXT(beginproblem());


############################################################
# PG Setup Code
############################################################
Context('Numeric');
$less  = DropDown(['<','>','='], 0, showInStatic => 0);
$more  = DropDown(['<','>','='], 1, showInStatic => 0);
$equal = DropDown(['<','>','='], 2, showInStatic => 0);

############################################################
# Body
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML
Choose the correct inequality or equal sign to make the relation true.

a.  Let [`x`] and [`y`] be integers such that [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}x \lt y\text{.}`]

    Then [`x - y`] [_]{$less}{5} [`y - x\text{.}`]


a.  Let [`x`] and [`y`] be integers, such that [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}1 \lt x \lt y\text{.}`]

    Then [`xy`] [_]{$more}{5} [`x + y\text{.}`]


a.  Let [`x`] and [`y`] be fractions such that [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}0 \lt x \lt y \lt 1\text{.}`]

    Then [`xy`] [_]{$less}{5} [`x + y\text{.}`]


a.  Let [`x`] and [`y`] be integers, such that [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}x \lt y\text{.}`]

    Then [`x + 2y`] [_]{$more}{5} [`2x + y\text{.}`]




END_PGML

############################################################
# Solution
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a.  Since [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}x \lt y\text{,}`] this means that [`x-y`] results in a negative number and [`y-x`] results in a positive number. Thus, [`x-y`] [$less] [`y-x\text{.}`]


a.  This problem comes down to looking at the impact of multiplying by [`y`] compared to the impact of adding [`y\text{.}`] Since [`\newcommand{\gt}{>}x\gt1\text{,}`] [`x`] is at least [`2\text{.}`] Similarly, [`y`] is at least [`3\text{.}`] Multiplying by [`y`] results in a bigger number than adding [`y\text{.}`]


a.  This problem comes down to looking at the impact of multiplying by [`y`] compared to the impact of adding [`y\text{.}`] Since [`\newcommand{\lt}{<}0 \lt y \lt 1\text{,}`] this means that [`y`] is a fraction between [`0`] and [`1\text{.}`] Thus, multiplying by [`y`] results in a smaller number, whereas adding [`y`] results in a bigger number.


a.  Notice that [`x + 2y`] and [`2x + y`] both have an [`x`] and a [`y\text{.}`] Let's subtract an [`x`] and a [`y`] from those expressions.

    [```\newcommand{\wonder}[1]{\stackrel{\text{?}}{#1}}\newcommand{\gt}{>}\newcommand{\amp}{&}
    \begin{aligned}
    x + 2y \amp \wonder{\gt} 2x + y\\
    x + 2y - x - y \amp \wonder{\gt} 2x + y - x - y\\
    y \amp \wonder{\gt} x
    \end{aligned}
    ```]

    Since [`\newcommand{\gt}{>}y \gt x\text{,}`] this means that [`\newcommand{\gt}{>}x + 2y \gt 2x + y\text{.}`]




END_PGML_SOLUTION

